course: dictionary
lesson: 9966
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some small words mean different things in different languages, and te is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. In a music lesson, «do re mi fa so la te do» is a list of notes, so here te is a note.
explain: In a French sentence, «Je te remercie» means I thank you, so te means you. In French, te is used for one friend or child, not for a grown-up.
explain: In New Zealand, «Te Reo Māori» means the Māori language, so te means the. Ask yourself what the words around it are doing, then check that your meaning still makes sense in the sentence.
fact: Te is the seventh note of the major scale, sung just below the top do.
fact: In the USA, the same note is spelt ti.
fact: In French, «te» means you when talking to one friend or child, as in «Je t'aime», where te becomes t'.
fact: In Māori, «te» means the for one thing, as in «Te Papa», the museum.
fact: Outside music, te is mostly a word from other languages, so context clues show you which meaning to use.
exercise: In «do re mi fa so la te do», which note comes just before the top do? | *Te | So | Fa | La
exercise: In French, «Je te remercie» means I thank you. What does te mean in that sentence? | the | music | *you | yes
exercise: «Te Papa» is a museum in New Zealand. What does te mean in «Te Papa»? | and | note | you | *the
test: Americans spell the seventh note of the scale with a different name. Which spelling do they use? | la | re | *ti | do
test: What does «Te Reo Māori» mean? | The New Zealand museum | *The Māori language | The music notes | The French language
exam: Mia reads «Je te remercie» and «Te Reo Māori» and wonders why te means different things. Why? | Because te is only ever a music note | Because te always means you | *Because te is a word from several languages, so the context shows which meaning fits | Because the spelling changes in each sentence
